sequenceDiagram
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, R1-R4, single-swamp-node (@swamp/ssh from 209), DECISIONS 1 (token minted at admission, vault.get), Schedules native (Nick 2026-11-02). docs/reference/instance-removal.md.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 3, 8, 14, 17, 22 (Route first, DNS only outside the TLD, cfgmgmt absent applies, no script read, source capture, report or assert step).
  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, the Route is withdrawn first, before teardown; a failed teardown leaves the site down and a rerun completes.
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): an Instance without a Route skips the Route withdrawal and the DNS records (a bare destroy spent 33 s and 11 s there on nothing), and a destroyed name waits no DNS grace (it has no new target to move to; the 31 s wait was 27% of a 116 s destroy).
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a destroy applies each published state once. Without the grace, the prepared, withdrawn and live Caddyfiles and the prepared and final DNS records are identical, so the Route mutation publishes each once (two of three Caddy publications and two of three DNS publications were repeats); the separate DNS step of the Instance, which repeated the mutation, is gone.
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Route withdrawal is one Ansible read, one composition and one Ansible apply per Node, the Nodes in parallel, in place of about 30 serial dispatches of the Route mutation (34 s of a 46 s destroy). The pool and Caddyfile composition is unchanged; it runs once against what the Node holds, and the captured states keep their revision checks. DNS records leave in the same phase as the Caddy site of another Node, not after it.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:destroy
  %% kind: instance.destroy
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: instance:destroy 42
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:destroy 42`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance> [--yes] [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Instance's id, as `instance:list` shows it
  %%> Confirmation:
  %%> - without `--yes`, the CLI asks first; the default is No
  %%> - without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--yes` is required
  %%> With `--force`:
  %%> - also deletes dirty or unpublished source, and a checkout with its linked worktrees
  %%> - it never waives the source identity checks, and it is not consent
  Note over CLI: Confirm removal
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - asks for default-No consent before sending the removal request
  %%> - `--yes` supplies that consent without the prompt; noninteractive and JSON calls require it
  opt --force
    Note over GW: Skip pushed-source checks
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - skips token minting and the git credential and pushed-work steps below
    %%> - permits dirty or unpublished source and linked worktrees, after source identity checks
    %%> - supplies no removal consent; the prompt or `--yes` is still required
  end
  Note over GW: Validate, capture the set<br/>Instance 42 · task #5132
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42
  %%> - Instance 42 is a development Instance; production removal is not charted here
  %%> - no other lifecycle change and no unfinished transfer holds Instance 42
  %%> Captures:
  %%> - the source: path, owner, origin, branch, linked worktrees
  %%> - what Instance 42 owns: Processes, Schedules, Route, SQLite files, server databases
  %%> - the Project teardown steps
  %%> Result:
  %%> - Instance 42 is marked removing, with the captured set
  %%> - task #5132 recorded with the full intent
  GW->>SW: Remove Schedule triggers<br/>workflow trigger removal
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the native Swamp schedule trigger of each Schedule of Instance 42
  %%> - there is no Node work here; Schedules install nothing on Nodes
  %%> Why first:
  %%> - no new fire starts against a checkout that is being removed
  %%> Running fire:
  %%> - a fire that already started runs to its end, within its own timeout, and is still recorded
  opt private repository
    GW->>SW: Push the token<br/>vault: github-token
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a normal removal checks that the work is pushed, which needs a fetch from origin
    %%> - only the Gateway holds the GitHub App key
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - mints a read token for this one repository, valid 1 hour, and pushes it into Swamp's vault
    %%> Not needed:
    %%> - with `--force`, or for a public repository
  end
  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state
  %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first
  %%> Not held:
  %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read
  %%> Limit:
  %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run
  %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled
  %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes
  %%> - not held: task provision and cancel, instance register and the Cluster Router writes, which publish sites too
  %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-destroy
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one run of `operation-instance-destroy` on Swamp serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  %%> - the captured set, the teardown steps, the Node's managed user
  %%> - secrets only as vault key names
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5132
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Write the git credential<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the vault token as a 0600 credential file, so the token never enters argv
  SW->>N: Check work is pushed<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · git fetch
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - for each checkout, fetches origin with that credential file, then compares with `git status --porcelain` and `git branch -r --contains HEAD`
  %%> Refuses:
  %%> - dirty source
  %%> - a `HEAD` that no origin branch or tag contains
  %%> - linked worktrees on the checkout
  %%> Result of a refusal:
  %%> - the run stops here and nothing changes
  %%> Why first:
  %%> - a fact that can change after admission, and every later step depends on it
  SW->>N: Remove the git credential<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent
  %%> When:
  %%> - always after the check, also when it refused
  opt Instance has a Route
  SW->>N: Withdraw the Route<br/>Ansible read, compose, apply
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the Caddy site of Instance 42, its PHP pool and its certificate, then reloads Caddy; no restart
  %%> - per Node, in parallel: one Ansible read of the pools, the Caddyfile and the private DNS files, one composition of the captured states against them, one Ansible apply
  %%> - the apply writes the pools, tests and reloads PHP-FPM, writes the Caddyfile, validates and reloads Caddy, writes the DNS records and restarts the backend, then removes the certificate folders
  %%> - for a Route domain outside the Cluster or Node TLD it also removes the private DNS records on Node 1 (private DNS answers the whole TLD, so a domain inside it has none), in this step and not in a step of its own
  %%> If it does not hold:
  %%> - every later step is skipped and the task fails with `route.withdrawal_failed`; the Instance and its source stay
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp runs the Ansible phases from 209 over SSH; the playbook ships with the run, and nothing is copied to the Node
  %%> - a live file that matches none of the captured states stops the phase before it writes (`owned_revision_conflict`)
  %%> - the apply refuses a file that changed since the read
  %%> Why first:
  %%> - unpublish before removing, so no request reaches a half-removed Instance
  %%> - accepted: a failed teardown leaves Instance 42 without its site until a rerun completes the removal
  %%> No DNS grace:
  %%> - the name is removed, not moved, so no client has a new target to switch to; the Route is withdrawn right after its DNS records go
  %%> Keeps:
  %%> - every other site, pool and certificate on Node 12
  end
  SW->>N: Stop, remove Processes<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd absent
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes each Process of Instance 42 as a `forEach`: `@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd` or `docker_container` with `ensure: absent`, as `process:destroy` does
  %%> Why before teardown:
  %%> - nothing still writes to the application while teardown runs
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - Processes of other Instances on Node 12
  SW->>N: Run teardown steps<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · in order
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - runs the Project's teardown steps in order, as the Node's managed user, from the repository root, one `@swamp/ssh exec` each
  %%> Limits:
  %%> - each step has its own timeout
  %%> Failure:
  %%> - a failed step ends the run with its own exit code; the next `instance:destroy` runs the whole list again
  opt SQLite files recorded
    SW->>N: Remove SQLite files<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - deletes the SQLite files that Instance 42 owns, by their recorded paths
    %%> - runs beside the database step; neither needs the other
  end
  opt owned databases
    SW->>N: Drop owned databases<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - drops each database and user that Instance 42 owns on a Database server, one `docker exec -i` per statement with the SQL on stdin
    %%> How:
    %%> - the admin login comes from the server's own vault key, as a 0600 option file for MySQL, local trust for Postgres, `AUTH` on stdin for Valkey
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - registered external databases; their records go with the Instance, nothing on their host changes
  end
  SW->>N: Remove hibernation files<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the awake and cold markers and the Caddy access log outlive the Instance otherwise
  SW->>N: Remove the source<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · worktree, rm
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - `git worktree remove --force` per linked worktree, then `rm -rf` of the checkout
  %%> - the paths come from the Gateway's records; a rerun converges
  %%> Runs as:
  %%> - the Node's own user, not root
  SW->>N: Check it is gone<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt check absent
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the `check` of the directory, file, systemd and `docker_container` behaviors with `ensure: absent`: checkout, units and containers are gone
  %%> Why:
  %%> - Instance 42 only counts as removed when the Node shows it; this is the one end check
  alt all steps pass
    N-->>SW: Instance 42 absent
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the check passed
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Delete Instance, records<br/>task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Instance 42 is deleted, with its Processes, Schedules and Route records
    %%> - owned database records are deleted
    %%> - closed transfer history stays, without its Instance link
    %%> - task #5132 succeeds
  else a step fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or refusal
    %%> When:
    %%> - the pushed check refuses, a teardown step fails, or something is still there
    %%> How:
    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code`
    Note over GW: Keep Instance, mark failed<br/>rerun the same destroy
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Instance 42 and its records stay, marked removal failed
    %%> - task #5132 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - what was already removed stays removed; no rollback
    %%> - the Route is already withdrawn, so the site stays down; a rerun of the same destroy completes the removal
    %%> - the Schedule triggers stay removed
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run `instance:destroy 42` again; it is a new task and converges the same captured set
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5132
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
